1. Disclaimer
-------------

We are not liable for any damage caused by the use of this program.

XMMS is NOT a port of windows95's WinAmp. We just borrowed the GUI! :)


2. Installation
---------------

XMMS is built with CGTK 1.4.0 or later and libsamplerate 0.1.9 or
later, which converts songs at other rates to the device's rate.
Building it from git also
needs GNU autoconf 2.71, automake 1.16, libtool, gettext's autopoint
and pkg-config. On Debian, autopoint is a package of its own that
gettext does not pull in.

The plugins are built when configure finds what they need:

   MPEG audio (Layers 1, 2 and 3)   libmpg123 1.25 or later, and zlib
   Ogg Vorbis                       libogg, libvorbis and libvorbisfile
   Opus                             libopusfile 0.12 or later
   AAC and ALAC (.m4a, .aac)        faad2 2.10 or later (ALAC is built in)
   https:// streams                 OpenSSL 1.1.1 or later, or LibreSSL
   FLAC                             libFLAC 1.2.0 or later
   Modules (mod, s3m, xm, ...)      libmikmod 3.1.5 or later
   ALSA output                      alsa-lib 0.9.0 or later
   sndio output                     libsndio
   OSS output                       <sys/soundcard.h>

The OpenGL spectrum analyzer draws in a GtkGLArea, so it works on
whatever window system CGTK does. It is built when CGTK has GtkGLArea,
as it does when built on a system with EGL, and the system has OpenGL's
library and <GL/gl.h>; --disable-opengl leaves it out.

2.1 Basic Installation
----------------------

autoreconf -vif
./configure
make
make install

This will put the binary in /usr/local/bin and plugins in
/usr/local/lib/xmms/

With glibc, the dynamic linker finds libraries in /usr/local/lib only
through its cache, so "make install" runs ldconfig when installing
directly as root. Otherwise (or after "make install LDCONFIG="), run
ldconfig by hand before starting xmms. For a prefix the dynamic linker
does not search, add its lib directory to /etc/ld.so.conf.d/ and run
ldconfig, or set LD_LIBRARY_PATH.

XMMS runs as the user who starts it. It no longer asks for realtime
scheduling and must not be installed setuid root.

2.2 Window Decorations
----------------------

XMMS asks the window system for windows without borders or title bars,
since the skin draws its own. Window managers that honour the hint
(all current ones do) show the skin alone; "Show window manager
decorations" in Preferences -> Options puts the manager's frame back
for those who want it. On Wayland the compositor leaves the window
undecorated.

2.3 Skin Installation
---------------------

XMMS creates a directory called ~/.local/share/xmms/Skins/ (see 2.5) in
which you just unarchive the skins the same way as you do for WinAmp.

However, you don't need to unarchive them since XMMS supports archived
skins. XMMS currently reads the following formats: wsz, zip, tar,
tar.gz and tgz

Just copy the archive to one of the skin paths and XMMS will take care
of the rest. XMMS reads the archive itself, with no unzip or tar, and
keeps only the skin's images and text files, in memory: nothing in the
archive is written to disk.

XMMS installs four skins of its own besides the built-in default,
Bloodrose, Innercity, Candyflip and Grand, in the system skin
directory (/usr/local/share/xmms/Skins when XMMS is installed under
/usr/local).

Use ALT+S when using XMMS to bring up the skin selector. XMMS will
remember which skin you had loaded when you start XMMS the next time.
In the skin selector you have an option to use random skin on play,
this will pick a random skin on song change.

XMMS looks for skins in these directories:

<prefix>/share/xmms/Skins
$XDG_DATA_HOME/xmms/Skins   (~/.local/share/xmms/Skins)

or you can set the variable SKINSDIR to another location of your choice.

for bash:
export SKINSDIR=/path/to/Skins:/more/paths/to/other/locations/of/Skins

for csh:
setenv SKINSDIR /path/to/Skins:/more/paths/to/other/locations/of/Skins

The Winamp Skin Museum keeps tens of thousands of Winamp 2 skins, all
of which XMMS can use:

   https://skins.webamp.org/

2.4 Wayland
-----------

On X11, XMMS's three windows are three windows that XMMS moves about
itself, docking them to one another. A Wayland compositor places
windows itself and tells a program nothing of where they are, so on
Wayland the player, equalizer and playlist are one window of the
compositor's, in a layout chosen in Preferences -> Options, or from
the main menu under Layout: the equalizer under the player and the
playlist to the right of them (the default), or the three one above
the other. The playlist is resized by its corner, as ever, by its
width in the first layout and by its height in the second, so that
the whole stays a rectangle; the compositor may resize the window the
same way. XMMS remembers the playlist's size in each layout. Hiding
or shading a window closes the gap it leaves. Keys go to whichever of
the three the pointer is over.

CGTK chooses the window system at run time; GDK_BACKEND=x11 or
GDK_BACKEND=wayland picks one when both are available.

2.5 Files
---------

XMMS keeps its files where the XDG Base Directory Specification puts
them: configuration under $XDG_CONFIG_HOME, which is ~/.config when it
is unset, and data under $XDG_DATA_HOME, ~/.local/share by default.

   ~/.config/xmms/config           configuration, the plugins' included
   ~/.config/xmms/xmms.m3u         the playlist loaded at startup
   ~/.config/xmms/menurc           key bindings for the menus
   ~/.config/xmms/gtkrc            XMMS-specific GTK settings
   ~/.config/xmms/eq.preset        equalizer presets
   ~/.config/xmms/eq.auto_preset   presets loaded automatically per song
   ~/.local/share/xmms/Skins       skins
   ~/.local/share/xmms/Plugins     plugins installed by the user

Earlier versions kept all of this in ~/.xmms. That directory is not
read; a new XMMS starts afresh. Its control socket, which the xmms
command line options and other programs talk to, is /tmp/xmms_USER.N
as it has always been.


3. Documentation
----------------

This file, and xmms(1).

3.1 Controlling XMMS
--------------------

When you start up XMMS, you will get a console very similar to that of
WinAmp.

- On the top is the window title bar. To the right you will see 3 buttons,
   Left button will minimize XMMS.
   Middle button will make XMMS only display the title bar.
   Right button will end the XMMS session.

- The area in the upper left part displays the following:
   - Play state: Paused, Stopped, or Playing
   - Time elapsed in the current song or if you click on it, the reversed.
   - Spectrum analyzer of the sound being played. Right mouse click will
     bring up the Visualization menu. Left mouse button will change the
     analyzer to an oscilloscope and/or none.

- To the right of the Spectrum analyzer is the title of the file being played.
  This also contains the length of the song being played, as well as its
  position in the [unsorted] playlist. Right clicking in this window will bring
  up a new menu with some more options that are self explaining.

- In the left part of the Spectrum analyzer you'll have letters (at least if
  you use the default skin) O A I D V  left mouse clicking on these will open
  up menus or perform actions.
  O : Options menu
  A : Always on top
  I : File info box
  D : Double size mode
  V : Visualization menu

- Underneath the track title are the following static informational data:
   - bit rate in KBps (usually 128 or 112)
   - Sample Rate in KHz (usually 44)
   - Stereo or Mono channel mixing

- Underneath the informational data are a few controls you can play with:
   - The first slider controls the volume (XMMS's own, not the mixer's)
   - The second slider controls the balance between speakers
   - The button marked "EQ" loads up the graphic equalizer
   - The button marked "PL" loads up the playlist editor
   - The LARGE slide bar moves from left to right as the song plays. You can
     drag this to jump to another location in the current file.

- On the bottom of the console are the standard buttons you would see on a CD
  player: Previous track, Play, Pause, Stop, Next track, eject, shuffle
  and repeat.

   - The eject button doesn't REALLY eject, of course. :) It opens up the
     file requester. The File Requester builds a playlist for the current
     XMMS session. You can use it to load files, add files to the list, or
     load all the songs in a directory.

   - The shuffle button randomizes the sequence of the playlist.

   - The repeat button when enabled makes the playlist loop when it reaches the
     end of the playlist.


3.1.1 Key bindings
------------------

Global: (Main, Equalizer and Playlist window)

  z = Previous song
  x = Play
  c = Pause
  v = Stop
  b = Next song
  l = Play file (brings up the Load file(s) dialog)
  j = Jump to file (in the existing playlist)
  r = Toggle Repeat
  s = Toggle Shuffle

  Shift + l = Play directory (brings up the Add Directory dialog)
  Shift + q = Clear the queue

  Control + l = Play location (url)
  Control + p = Preferences dialog
  Control + v = Visualization plugin dialog
  Control + r = Time remaining
  Control + a = Always on top
  Control + s = Show on all desktops
  Control + w = Winshade mode
  Control + d = Doublesize mode
  Control + e = Easy move
  Control + j = Jump to time
  Control + z = Start of list
  Control + n = No Playlist Advance
  Control + 3 = File info dialog
  Control + q = Quit

  Control + Alt + w = Toggle Equalizer winshade mode
  Shift + Control + w = Toggle Playlist winshade mode
  Shift + Control + l = Time display in minutes and seconds (MMM:SS)

  Alt + w = Toggle mainwindow
  Alt + e = Toggle playlist window
  Alt + g = Toggle equalizer window
  Alt + s = Skin selector
  F5      = Reload skin

Main window:

  Arrow key up    = Volume up 2%
  Arrow key down  = Volume down 2%
  Arrow key right = Skip 5 seconds forward in song
  Arrow key left  = Skip 5 seconds back in song

Playlist window:

  Arrow key up    = up one step in playlist
  Arrow key down  = Down one step in playlist

  Delete          = Remove selected songs from playlist
  Control+ Delete = Keeps the selected tracks and removes the rest
  Q               = Queue/Dequeue the tracks selected
  Shift + Q       = Clear the queue list
  Alt + Q         = Queue manager
  Page Up         = Move one page up
  Page Down       = Move one page down
  Home            = Go to the first song
  End             = Go to the last song
  Enter           = Play selected song
  Insert          = Add file dialog
  Shift + Insert  = Add directory dialog
  Alt + Insert    = Add url dialog

Equalizer window:

  `               = Preamp up
  Tab             = Preamp down
  1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 0 = Band 1 to 10 up
  q w e r t y u i o p = Band 1 to 10 down

  The modifier combinations above (Control + e, Control + r, Alt + e
  and so on) keep their global meaning in the equalizer window.

Equalizer shade mode:

  Arrow key up    = Volume up 2%
  Arrow key down  = Volume down 2%
  Arrow key right = Balance 4% to right
  Arrow key left  = Balance 4% to left


3.2 Playlist editor
-------------------

To access the Playlist editor, select the button labeled "PL" on the right
side of the XMMS console.

This will bring up the actual playlist window, here you'll find 5 buttons.
All of these buttons can be held down to bring up an extra menu.
From left to right:

file +    : will add a file to current playlist, held down mode you'll have
            2 extra options
dir       : will let you pick a directory (recursive)
url       : will let you add an url for streaming

file -    : will delete the highlighted file, held down mode you'll have 3
            more options
crop      : delete all files except the highlighted in the list
all       : delete all files in the list
misc      : remove by extension, remove the entries whose files no longer
            exist, or physically delete the selected files from disk

sel all   : select all files in current playlist, held down mode you'll have
            2 extra options
sel zero  : select none
inv sel   : invert you selection

misc opts : held down you'll have 2 extra options
fileinfo  : opens the file info dialog.
sort      : release button on this will bring up another menu with sort options

load list : will let you pick a playlist to load, held down you'll have
            2 extra options
save      : will let you save your playlist
new       : will empty the playlist and let you create a new playlist

If you want to select/deselect files in the filerequester/playlist editor use
CTRL for files and SHIFT key for blocks of files. You can also browse the PL
using the cursor keys and enter to select song. Pressing the delete button will
remove the song from the playlist. If your mouse is equipped with a mouse
wheel, you can use this to scroll up and down.

Playlists are saved as .m3u or .pls, by the extension you give the file.
With "Use meta-data in playlists" (3.5.5) an .m3u carries each song's
title and length, so it loads without reading the files again.

3.3. Equalizer
--------------

To access the Equalizer, select the button labeled "EQ" on the right
side of the XMMS console.

That will bring up the Equalizer window. It looks like an equalizer on a stereo
and behaves like one as well. Press the button labeled ON to enable the use of
the equalizer, once you turned it on you use it as a normal equalizer.

The equalizer is part of XMMS itself and works on everything that is
played, whichever input plugin decodes it. (In 1.2.11 only the MPEG
audio plugin had one.) Each slider sets the gain at its band's centre
frequency and the response runs smoothly between them.

EQ presets are saved in ~/.config/xmms (see 2.5) when you close XMMS. You
can also have your own presets for different song using the "Preset"
button, XMMS can also import/export from WinAmp's preset files.

If 'Auto' is enabled, XMMS will try to load equalizer presets like this:

1: Look for a preset file in the directory of the file we are about to play.
2: Look for a directory preset file in the same directory.
3: Look for a preset saved with the "auto-load" feature.
4: Finally, try to load the "default" preset.

The 'preset' button will open up a menu with the following options:

Load
   Preset               : Will open a window with all available presets.
   Auto-load preset     : Will open a window with all available auto-load
                          presets.
   Default              : Will load the default preset.
   Zero                 : Will reset the equalizer to zero.
   From file            : Will load from a .preset file
   From WinAMP EQF file : Will load from a WinAMP equalizer file. If you
                          choose a library file only the first entry will
                          be loaded.
Import
   WinAMP presets       : Imports the presets contained in an WinAMP equalizer
                          library file (often named WINAMP.q1) and add all
                          the entries to the Preset window.
Save
   Preset               : Let you name the current preset and save it.
   Auto-load preset     : Saves the current settings as a preset for the song
                          currently playing.
   Default              : Saves the default value for the equalizer.
   To file              : Saves the current settings in a preset file.
   To WinAMP EQF file   : Exports the current settings to a file readable by
                          WinAMP.
Delete
   Preset               : Let you delete a preset from the list.
   Auto-load preset     : Let you delete a auto-load preset from the list.

Configure Equalizer     : Change the default names of directory based
                          preset files.

3.4 Menu
--------

There are several menu hot spots on the XMMS window. One place is at the left
hand side of the visual window described in sections 3.1 If you click the right
mouse button in the main window, the menus will also pop up (same as clicking
the button on the top left corner).

3.5 Preferences
---------------

Use the menu to open Options / Preferences or press CTRL-P to bring the
preferences dialog up.

The first four tabs are for different types of plugins, the plugins specific
configuration and usage will be explained in the plugins section of this
manual.

3.5.1 Audio I/O Plugins
-----------------------

In the 'Audio I/O' tab you control the heart of XMMS, which would be the Input
and Output plugins.

In the 'Input plugins' part you can disable/enable and configure the available
plugins. The list box displays the name of the plugin, it's file name and
'(disabled)' if you have disabled the plugin.

The 'Output Plugin' is where you tell XMMS how it should play the audio back.
Today you can only have one 'Output Plugin' active at a time. So you can't
both listen to the music through the 'ALSA Output' and save the music to disk
with 'Disk Writer'. Changing the output plugin takes effect when the next
song starts.

3.5.2 Effect Plugins
--------------------

Effect plugins can alter the sound in different ways.

3.5.3 General Plugins
---------------------

General plugins are mostly for controling XMMS.

3.5.4 Visualization Plugins
---------------------------

The 'Visualization' tab controls which visual effects you want to see when XMMS
is playing your music. See section 3.6.5 for the plugins shipped with XMMS.


3.5.5 Options
-------------

Here you change most of XMMS's behavior and settings.

'Read info on'
   tells XMMS when to load the information from the files in your playlist.
   'demand' will load the information when the files are visible in the
   playlist. 'load' will load the information when you add the file to the
   playlist. Having 'load' turned on when loading a 6000 song playlist might
   not be a good idea. 'play' loads the information only when the song is
   played.

'Allow multiple instances'
   This will allow you to start more than one XMMS. You have to quit XMMS
   before this change takes effect.

'Convert %20 to space'
   This will convert "%20" to " " when the filename is displayed in the
   playlist. (%20 is what browsers use instead of space)

'Always show clutterbar'
   Will make the OAIDV part of the main window to be displayed all the time.

'Convert underscore to space'
   This will display " " instead of "_" in the playlist.

'Save window positions'
   Will save the main window's position on the screen instead of letting the
   windowmanager choose location.

'Dim titlebar when inactive'
   Will tell XMMS to use the dimmed titlebar from the current skin when
   the window is inactive.

'Show numbers in playlist'
   Enables the display of the internal track number in the playlist.

'Sort "Jump to file" alphabetically'
   Will sort the 'Jump to file' dialog (available by pressing "j") in
   alphabetical order instead of the order it's in the playlist.

'Equalizer doublesize linked'
   Will display the Equalizer in doublesize if you make XMMS doublesize.

'Smooth title scroll'
   Makes the title scroll go smoother.

'Pause between songs for [ ] seconds'
   Will make a defined pause between each song.

'Layout' (Wayland)
   How the player, equalizer and playlist are arranged in the one window
   they make together: the playlist beside the player, or below the
   equalizer. See 2.4.

'Snap windows at [ ] pixels' (X11)
   Tells XMMS how close you can position the three windows until they dock /
   snap together with each other, and to the edges of the screen.

'Show window manager decorations' (X11)
   Enables the windowmanager borders around XMMS.

'Use "\" as a directory delimiter'
   This will come in handy if you are using playlist files from a windows
   player, so XMMS will treat \ as /.

'Mouse Wheel adjusts Volume by (%)'
   If you have a mouse with a wheel, you can change how much of the volume to
   be changed when you move it up or down.

'Use meta-data in playlists'
   When enabled, all playlists will be saved with the configured 'Title'
   string for quick loading of information when you load a playlist.


3.5.6 Fonts
-----------

Here you can change the fonts XMMS use. CGTK draws text with FreeType
from the fonts fontconfig knows, so fonts are named as in "Sans 10" or
"DejaVu Sans Bold 11"; the Browse buttons open a font selector.

'Playlist'
   Lets you choose what font XMMS should use for the playlist.

'Use the font below instead of the skin's'
   Makes XMMS draw the scrolling title in the main window with the chosen
   font instead of the skin's bitmap font.


3.5.7 Title
-----------

The 'Title format:' box allows you to alter in which order the information
about the current song is displayed.

Example: %p - %t (%a) [%y]
will display something like "Laibach - Alle Gegen Alle (Nato) [1994]"

Available fields are:

%p - Artist          (ex: Laibach)
%a - Album           (ex: Nato)
%g - Genre           (ex: Electronic)
%f - File name       (ex: laibach-allegegenalle)
%F - File path       (ex: /home/thomas/mp3)
%e - File extension  (ex: mp3)

%t - Track name      (ex: Alle Gegen Alle)
%n - Track number    (ex: 6)
%d - Date            (ex: ???)
%y - Year            (ex: 1994)
%c - Comment         (ex: Cover of D.A.F)

These fields makes use of normal printf(3) formatting codes.

Examples:
  %0.3n     Will pad the track number with 0's until it's 3 chars long.
  %30p      Prefix-pads the artist name to 30 chars length with spaces.
  %-30      Same as previous, but suffix instead.
  %-30.30p  Suffix-pad to 30 chars and "cut" to 30 too.


3.6 Plugins
-----------
Plugins is what makes XMMS work, by moving most of the code out of XMMS and
into a plugin architecture it's possible to change almost everything in XMMS.
There are today 5 different types of plugins. The plugins are named as
Winamp named its: in_*.so for input, out_*.so for output, dsp_*.so for
effects, gen_*.so for general and vis_*.so for visualization plugins.
They are installed in <prefix>/lib/xmms/, and plugins of your own go in
~/.local/share/xmms/Plugins.


3.6.1 Input plugins
-------------------
The input plugins are what you use to play mp3, ogg, flac, m4a, wav, mod
and so on. Every input plugin reads its format from a file or from an
http or https stream; the streaming code is shared and is described
first.


3.6.1.1 Streaming
-----------------
Give XMMS a URL (Play Location, Control+L, or the playlist's url button)
and the input plugin for the format fetches it. http:// and https://
are supported, the latter when XMMS was built with OpenSSL or LibreSSL;
a secure stream that redirects to an insecure one is refused. SHOUTcast
and Icecast servers are supported, with the title of the current track
when the server sends it. .pls and .m3u playlists fetched by URL are
read like local ones.

The streaming settings are shared: the 'Streaming' tab in any input
plugin's configuration shows the same settings, and a change made in one
holds for all:

'Buffer size (kb)' and 'Pre-buffer (percent)'
   How much of the stream XMMS keeps ahead of the decoder, and how much
   of that buffer must fill before playing starts.

'Use proxy'
   The proxy server to fetch through, with authentication if it needs it.

'Save stream to disk'
   Writes what is played to a file in the chosen directory, named after
   the stream. If the name is taken, -N is appended.

'Enable SHOUT/Icecast title streaming'
   Asks the server for the titles it sends along with the stream, and
   shows them as the song's title.


3.6.1.2 MPEG Layer 1/2/3 player
-------------------------------
The main reason why this player exists today is mp3 files, so what could be
better than a plugin that plays them?

It decodes with the system's libmpg123 and handles MPEG Layer 1/2/3 files
and VBR (variable bit rate) MP3 files, from disk or from streams.

In the 'Decoder' tab you choose the resolution, channels and down
sampling. If you have an older soundcard and the music is going half
speed, change the 'Resolution' setting.

In the 'Options' section you can tell XMMS how to handle the files you feed it.
You can tell the plugin to detect files by extension, content and both. The
reason why this option exists is to make XMMS less demanding when you add a lot
of MP3 files with the extension .mp3. If you use both then XMMS will first look
at the extension and then try to detect by content. This is useful if you have
CDs with music that aren't correctly named.

The 'Streaming' tab is described in 3.6.1.1.

In the 'Title' tab you can change the way XMMS presents the mp3 files to the
playlist.

ID3 is data stored in the mp3 file and can include Artist, Album etc. If you
uncheck 'Use ID3 tags' XMMS will display the filename instead of the ID3
information.

ID3V2 allows for a lot of extra data to be stored in the mp3 file, and doesn't
suffer from the limitations of ID3V1. XMMS uses the same data that ID3V1 has.
The 'file info' editor edits it in the ID3V2 tag, which it adds if the file
has none, and keeps the rest of what the tag holds, such as cover art. An ID3V1
tag the file also has is brought up to date with it, cut to ID3V1's lengths.
If you want XMMS to show the ID3V1 tag instead, turn on 'Disable ID3V2 tags'.

'Override generic titles' is used if you do not want to use the generic titles
defined in the preferences. See section 3.5.7.

The 'ID3 format:' box allows you to alter in which order the information about
the current song is displayed. It takes the same fields as the title
format in 3.5.7.


3.6.1.3 Ogg Vorbis player
-------------------------
Plays Ogg Vorbis files and streams, see https://xiph.org/vorbis/ for
more information.

The 'Title' tab works as the MPEG audio plugin's. The 'ReplayGain' tab
applies the REPLAYGAIN_* tags an encoder may have written: by track or
by album, with clipping prevention, and optionally a 6 dB boost with
hard limiting. The boost is not applied to files without ReplayGain
tags.


3.6.1.4 Opus player
-------------------
Plays Opus files and streams (.opus) with libopusfile, see
https://opus-codec.org/. The output gain in the file's header is
always applied; the 'Gain' setting chooses on top of that between no
normalisation, the R128 track gain, or the R128 album gain, and whether
to raise the result by 5 dB to ReplayGain's reference level.


3.6.1.5 FLAC player
-------------------
Plays FLAC files and streams with libFLAC, see https://xiph.org/flac/.

'Tag Handling' chooses whether the file name is always the title, and
'Title' overrides the generic title format as in the other plugins.
'ReplayGain' applies the tags by track or by album, with a preamp and
optional 6 dB hard limiting, and 'Output' chooses the resolution and
dithering: FLAC files of 24 bits per sample can be dithered to 16, with
noise shaping of your choice.


3.6.1.6 MPEG-4 Audio player
---------------------------
Plays AAC and Apple Lossless (ALAC) in MP4 files (.m4a, .m4b, .mp4), and
AAC in ADTS files and streams (.aac). AAC is decoded by faad2; the ALAC
decoder is built in. Playback is gapless when the file carries the
encoder delay and length, in an iTunSMPB tag or an edit list.
'Override generic titles' and the MP4 tag format work as in the other
plugins.


3.6.1.7 Wave player
-------------------
This plugin plays as suggested, wave files: PCM of 8, 12, 16, 24 and 32
bits, 32 and 64 bit floating point, and A-law and mu-law.


3.6.1.8 MikMod player
---------------------
This plugin will play a numerous older "module" formats supported by
the MikMod library (libmikmod): 669, amf, dsm, far, gdm, imf, it, m15,
med, mod, mtm, s3m, stm, stx, ult and xm.

In the first configuration tab you can choose in which quality mikmod
should play the modules. If you have an older soundcard you might want to
change the defaults to match your card. If the modules are played in what
seems to be half speed change 'Resolution' to 8 bit.

In the 'Options' tab you have five options. 'Look for hidden patterns in
modules' can be used to play "patterns" which exist in the modules, but
wouldn't be called when played. (Since most module formats decide on a
pattern basis which the next pattern should be, you can "hide" smaller tunes
within a larger module.)

'Use surround mixing' will as hinted turn on some mixing that gives you an
surround effect. This is however only usable if you're playing the module
in stereo.

'Force volume fade...' will fade out the music when the modules reaches
it's end.

'Use interpolation' can enhance the sound of the samples contained in the
modules, unless you're really out for that old crispy retro feeling. :)

'Default panning separation' "The original Amiga formats had an implicit
channel allocation (because the Amiga's hardware had same), 100% left or 100%
right depending on the track number (left right right left, or right left left
right, I forget). That sounded OK when they were written because most monitors
had only a single speaker, and the flash monitors didn't separate the speakers
more than about a foot, but when you try something like that with your speakers
in opposite corners of the room it drives you to distraction. That control
lessens the annoyance for file formats that don't specify the balance."

- Simon Hosie


3.6.1.9 Tone Generator
----------------------
Will generate a sinus tone or a mix of several sinus tones. To use it add a
URL: tone://frequency1;frequency2;frequency3;...

Example: tone://2000

will play a 2000Hz tone.

Example: tone://2000;2005

will play a 2000Hz tone and a 2005Hz tone at the same time. Notice the 5Hz
beats between the two tones.

The use of this baffles me, but at least I can use it to annoy my neighbors
dog.


3.6.2 Output plugins
--------------------
This type of plugins is what is used to send the audio data to your soundcard
or alternative devices.

The audio pipeline is XMMS's own: the input plugins hand it decoded
audio, it runs the equalizer and the effect plugins over it, and one
thread owns the output plugin and feeds it. Volume and balance are
XMMS's too, applied as the audio leaves for the device, so they work
the same through every output plugin and never touch a hardware mixer;
the percentages follow the same curve as PulseAudio and PipeWire, so
50% is about -18 dB. If the device is busy when a song starts, XMMS
waits for it rather than failing.

XMMS keeps the device open while it runs. The output plugin's
configuration ('Sample rate') says which rate to ask the device for,
44100 Hz unless chosen otherwise, or 'Each song's rate'. A device that
can't run at the rate asked for runs at the nearest it can: one shared
with other programs, such as ALSA's default device through dmix, runs
at the rate it was set up with, often 48000 Hz. 'Device runs at' shows
the rate it really runs at. Songs at any other rate are converted to
it with libsamplerate, so the device is not opened again between
songs, and nothing is lost or added where songs join, whatever their
rates. With 'Each song's rate' the device is opened again at each
song's rate, and a song at the rate the device runs at is played as it
is. 'Conversion' is 'Best' unless set to 'Medium', which costs a
quarter to a third as much CPU, for very high rates or slow machines.
The Disk Writer always
writes each song at its own rate.


3.6.2.1 ALSA Output
-------------------
Plays through ALSA, the Linux sound system, and so through PipeWire or
PulseAudio when their ALSA plugin is the default device.

'Audio device' is the PCM to open, "default" unless you choose a card,
with the 'Sample rate' below it.
'Advanced settings' sets the buffer and period times of the device, and
the time XMMS itself buffers ahead of it, in milliseconds. Changes take
effect from the next song.


3.6.2.2 OSS Driver
------------------
For systems with the Open Sound System or an emulation of it: FreeBSD,
and Linux kernels with OSS emulation. It uses the system's
<sys/soundcard.h>.

In the 'Devices' tab you can change the soundcard which XMMS is going to use,
and the 'Sample rate'.
If your driver have more than one dsp, you can change the one XMMS uses by
enabling 'Use alternate device' and changing the '/dev/dsp' to suit your needs.

In the 'Buffering' tab you can change how much data the OSS plugin will buffer.
The 'Buffer size' ranges from 200 - 10000ms. If you want the plugin to wait for
the buffer to be filled before it starts playing the music change the 'Pre-
buffer' value, this ranges from 0% - 90% of the 'Buffer size' value.


3.6.2.3 sndio Output
--------------------
Plays through sndio, the OpenBSD sound system, which is also available on
other systems. "default" plays through sndiod when it is running, and
otherwise straight to the first audio device; AUDIODEVICE, if set, is
used instead. Its configuration also has the 'Sample rate'. Changes
take effect from the next song.


3.6.2.4 Disk Writer
-------------------
The 'Disk Writer' plugin will only output the music into a .wav file in
the 'Path' you set in it's configuration window. Effect plugins and the
equalizer are applied to what is written. 'Don't strip file name
extension' names the output song.mp3.wav rather than song.wav.


3.6.3 Effect plugins
--------------------
Effect plugins can alter the sound of the music you are listening to.


3.6.3.1 Echo
------------
Adds an echo effect to the audio.

In the configuration you can change 'Delay' 'Feedback' 'Volume' and enable
'Surround Echo'


3.6.3.2 Extra Stereo
--------------------
Enhances the stereo effect on the audio.

In the configuration screen you can choose intensity.


3.6.3.3 Voice removal
---------------------
A simple voice removal plugin, no configuration.


3.6.4 General plugins
---------------------
Mostly used for controlling XMMS and passing data to other programs.


3.6.4.1 Joystick Control
------------------------
This plugin allows you to control XMMS from one or two joysticks,
/dev/input/js0 and /dev/input/js1 by default. The configuration is
rather straight forward and since I don't have a joystick, I'll leave it
up you to figure it out.


3.6.4.2 Song Change
-------------------
This plugin will run a defined program each time you change song in XMMS.

In the configuration you can define what will happen when the song changes,
toward the end of a song, or when the Playlist reaches the end.

Available information you might want to pass to the program are:

   %F - frequency (in hertz)
   %c - number of channels
   %f - filename (full path)
   %l - length (in milliseconds)
   %n or %s - song name
   %r - rate (in bits per second)
   %t - playlist position (%02d)
   %p - currently playing (1 or 0)

example: echo "%n" >>~/xmms_log

will fill your ~/xmms_log file with all the songs you have listened to.


3.6.5 Visualization plugins
---------------------------
Eye candy plugins.


3.6.5.1 Blur scope
-------------------
A simple blurring oscilloscope, in the configuration you can change the color
with the standard GTK color dialog.


3.6.5.2 OpenGL Spectrum analyzer
--------------------------------
Displays a rotating spectrum analyzer disc, in a window of its own, drawn
in step with the display. To close it press the Escape key on your
keyboard.

The "z x c v b" keys are bound to the same as it is in the main window of XMMS.
Use the arrow keys to change the X and Z axis, and Q + W to change the Y axis.

Pressing Enter will change back to the default settings.


3.6.5.3 Simple Spectrum analyzer
--------------------------------
Displays the oh-so-standard green-yellow-red spectrum bars. No configuration.


4. Command Line Options
-----------------------

xmms --help will produce:

Usage: xmms [options] [files] ...

Options:
--------

  -h, --help                    Display this text and exit.
  -n, --session                 Select XMMS session (Default: 0)
  -r, --rew                     Skip backwards in playlist
  -p, --play                    Start playing current playlist
  -u, --pause                   Pause current song
  -s, --stop                    Stop current song
  -t, --play-pause              Pause if playing, play otherwise
  -f, --fwd                     Skip forward in playlist
  -e, --enqueue                 Don't clear the playlist
  -Q, --queue                   Add file(s) to playlist and queue

  -S, --toggle-shuffle[=SWITCH] Toggle the 'shuffle' flag.
  -R, --toggle-repeat[=SWITCH]  Toggle the 'repeat' flag.
  -A, --toggle-advance[=SWITCH] Toggle the 'no playlist advance' flag.

                                SWITCH may be either 'on' or 'off'

  -m, --show-main-window        Show the main window.
  -v, --version                 Print version number and exit.
  -q, --quit                    Close remote session.

You can specify files on the command line, e.g:
xmms file1.mp3 file2.mp3 file3.mp3
xmms *.mp3
xmms playlist.m3u (note: playlists must be named .m3u or .pls)

If you do this while XMMS is running the current playlist
will be cleared and the files/playlist specified on the command
line will be used instead.

To keep the current playlist intact use the -e option. For more
information type 'man xmms'.

Other programs control a running XMMS through libxmms (xmmsctrl.h),
which xmms-config and xmms.pc describe.

5. Features
-----------

5.1 Supported File formats
-------------------------

MPEG audio: MP1, MP2 and MP3, files and streams
Ogg Vorbis
Opus
FLAC
AAC in MP4 (.m4a, .m4b) and ADTS (.aac), and Apple Lossless
WAV: PCM, floating point, A-law and mu-law

module formats:
mod, xm, s3m, med, it
669, amf, dsm, far
gdm, imf, m15, mtm
stm, stx, ult, uni

others:
SHOUTcast and Icecast streams over http and https
m3u and pls playlists, local or by URL
tone:// test tones


5.2 Supported Features
----------------------

Seeking in files
Volume/Balance
Shuffle play
Repeat play
Play queue, with a queue manager
Playlist editor
Spectrum Analyzer
One Line mode al'a WinShade in WinAmp
Oscilloscope
Timer Elapsed/Timer Remaining
Plug-in system Output/Input/Effect/General/Visualization
Ten band equalizer on every format, with presets and auto-load presets
ReplayGain for Vorbis, Opus and FLAC
Double Size option
WinAmp 2.0 skin support (can use wsz files)
GTK Requesters (with theme support)
Streaming/Shoutcast(1.0/1.1)/Icecast support, over https too
Runs on X11 and on Wayland
Auto remove borders if the WM has support for it
Fast jump in playlist
Scroll wheel support
Save to wav option
Saves http streams to HD
HTTP authentication
Proxy authentication support
Compiles and works on other UNIX's


6. Obtaining XMMS
-------------------
XMMS is released together with CGTK, the Classic GUI Toolkit, which
it is built against.

XMMS uses Winamp 2 skins (.wsz files). The Winamp Skin Museum keeps
tens of thousands of them:

   https://skins.webamp.org/


7. Misc
-------

7.1. Playing streams from a browser
-----------------------------------

"make install" installs xmms.desktop, which registers XMMS for the audio
types it plays and for audio/x-scpls and audio/x-mpegurl, the .pls and
.m3u playlists that stream directories hand out. Choose XMMS when the
browser asks what to open them with, and it will play the stream; with
"Allow multiple instances" off, the running XMMS takes the list.

7.2. Tips and Tricks
-------------------

If you have a windows partition with WinAmp installed, a good idea would be to
set the SKINSDIR variable to that dir.

XMMS features some command line options like next/previous songs, those
things can be bound to a key in your desktop's keyboard settings: bind
one key to "xmms -r" and another to "xmms -f", and so on.

If you want all your mp3's in one playlist an easy way is:
locate .mp3 > /path/to/playlistname

(considering you have a fairly recent updatedb, don't blame us if locate don't
find the file you downloaded 3 minutes ago)

